1
00:00:08,140 --> 00:00:09,718
Rym: It's Tuesday, December 26th, 2023.

2
00:00:09,718 --> 00:00:09,919
Rym: I'm Rym.

3
00:00:13,274 --> 00:00:13,625
Scott: I'm Scott.

4
00:00:14,030 --> 00:00:15,296
Rym: And this is GeekNights.

5
00:00:15,336 --> 00:00:18,430
Rym: Tonight we are talking about Lethal Company.

6
00:00:19,130 --> 00:00:20,048
Scott: Let's do this.

7
00:00:20,930 --> 00:00:28,110
Rym: So, as many of you know, I, uh, I, and a small contingent of people, am a Zelda 2 enjoyer.

8
00:00:28,250 --> 00:00:31,270
Rym: Now, I want to be clear, I'm not saying Zelda 2 is a perfect game.

9
00:00:31,530 --> 00:00:36,190
Rym: Like, Zelda 2 has a number of flaws, but I like Zelda 2 as a game.

10
00:00:36,493 --> 00:00:37,744
Scott: I like parts of Zelda 2.

11
00:00:37,744 --> 00:00:38,390
Scott: I like the dungeons.

12
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:39,917
Rym: I've beaten it many times.

13
00:00:40,058 --> 00:00:42,070
Rym: Like, I know Zelda 2 very well.

14
00:00:42,250 --> 00:00:46,390
Rym: I can beat it in a pretty reasonable span of time with very few deaths.

15
00:00:46,630 --> 00:00:47,597
Rym: Like, I'm really good at it.

16
00:00:47,899 --> 00:00:49,250
Rym: Just a game I played a lot as a kid.

17
00:00:51,132 --> 00:00:58,210
Rym: Uh, and making the rounds on the internet recently was, people were calling it an HD remake, but it's not really that.

18
00:00:58,370 --> 00:01:09,009
Rym: Like, it's basically some fan, uh, at hoverbat.itch.io, and it looks like their name is Ziaol, Z-I-I-A-O-L.

19
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:15,310
Rym: Made, it's not a remake, it's not an HD, it's more like a remix.

20
00:01:16,030 --> 00:01:22,430
Rym: Like, they took Zelda 2, and they just made a more Zelda 2 Zelda 2 with a lot more stuff in it.

21
00:01:22,851 --> 00:01:26,550
Rym: And a lot more mechanics, and better dungeons, and better graphics.

22
00:01:26,671 --> 00:01:27,738
Rym: They made it widescreen.

23
00:01:27,758 --> 00:01:28,583
Rym: It runs on the PC.

24
00:01:28,603 --> 00:01:29,609
Rym: Like, they added all this stuff.

25
00:01:30,531 --> 00:01:36,769
Rym: And I was very skeptical of that kind of project, but a few people I trust said some good things about it.

26
00:01:37,370 --> 00:01:40,300
Scott: Did they do the smart thing where it's like, you don't announce it, right?

27
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:41,785
Scott: You just, until it's done, right?

28
00:01:43,570 --> 00:01:45,507
Scott: If Nintendo can't take this down, this exists forever.

29
00:01:45,990 --> 00:01:48,556
Scott: It's like, I've made something that Nintendo will shut down.

30
00:01:48,577 --> 00:01:53,709
Scott: I will wait until it is 100% done, then release it, and if they shut it down, people have it.

31
00:01:53,749 --> 00:01:54,050
Scott: We're good.

32
00:01:54,331 --> 00:01:54,531
Rym: Yep.

33
00:01:55,072 --> 00:02:04,350
Rym: So, this is an interesting case that I struggled with, because on one hand, it is really good.

34
00:02:05,491 --> 00:02:13,110
Rym: But on the other hand, it is so jankily implemented that I am unwilling to put forth the effort to play it.

35
00:02:13,912 --> 00:02:16,150
Rym: So, instead I watched a long play of someone playing it.

36
00:02:16,270 --> 00:02:18,350
Scott: How could it be good and jank at the same time?

37
00:02:18,550 --> 00:02:19,493
Rym: So, it's fascinating.

38
00:02:19,834 --> 00:02:25,550
Rym: The person who made this is an excellent game designer, in my opinion.

39
00:02:26,150 --> 00:02:36,470
Rym: They are also, they understood exactly how Zelda II worked to a fault in excruciating detail, because this wasn't just like a ROM hack.

40
00:02:36,890 --> 00:02:40,250
Rym: They remade it from scratch in fucking Game Maker.

41
00:02:40,930 --> 00:02:42,062
Scott: Well, that's why it's jank.

42
00:02:42,123 --> 00:02:42,749
Scott: It's a Game Maker.

43
00:02:42,890 --> 00:02:45,895
Rym: And yet, the game itself is not jank.

44
00:02:46,215 --> 00:02:47,116
Rym: It is perfect.

45
00:02:47,217 --> 00:02:55,910
Rym: I played enough of it in a, like I had to hack together some bullshit to make it work, but when I got it to work, it feels exactly like Zelda II.

46
00:02:56,410 --> 00:02:59,336
Rym: Like, if you told me it was a ROM, I would have believed you.

47
00:02:59,396 --> 00:03:05,928
Rym: I would have been impressed and surprised, and I would have thought it was impossible to do that with a ROM, but it feels 100% perfect.

48
00:03:07,050 --> 00:03:13,310
Rym: The timing, the way the jumping works, the way the enemies behave, every little detail of the gameplay is perfect.

49
00:03:13,710 --> 00:03:27,870
Rym: They got the rules of how Zelda II plays in effect absolutely perfect in Game Maker, which I think is, that is an impressive feat, my person who made this, because Game Maker is not known for making very precise games.

50
00:03:27,930 --> 00:03:34,730
Rym: Especially a game that someone who has played it a lot would notice even the slightest problem with the implementation.

51
00:03:35,592 --> 00:03:38,697
Rym: And the design of the game itself, the new dungeons, just better.

52
00:03:39,158 --> 00:03:40,401
Rym: The items, just better.

53
00:03:40,481 --> 00:03:45,390
Rym: The way the spells work, like, it is a better game in almost every conceivable way.

54
00:03:45,530 --> 00:03:46,578
Rym: It's not a tall order, though.

55
00:03:48,610 --> 00:04:03,570
Rym: No, but it's not just that they added quality of life stuff, like there's a subway that connects all the cities you visited and fast travel and stuff, but like, there's just better quests and the dungeons are way more complex, the aesthetic is better, like it's great.

56
00:04:04,390 --> 00:04:14,530
Rym: However, because it's Game Maker, if you want to play this thing, you have to download an .exe file from itch.io, and this .exe file has a few issues.

57
00:04:14,710 --> 00:04:18,444
Rym: So number one, Windows is going to tell you it's a virus, but I don't think it is.

58
00:04:18,464 --> 00:04:19,870
Scott: I did run it.

59
00:04:20,230 --> 00:04:22,570
Scott: It tells you literally any .exe you downloaded says it's a virus.

60
00:04:23,652 --> 00:04:23,752
Rym: Yup.

61
00:04:24,194 --> 00:04:30,269
Rym: I only trusted this on a computer that has some controls and containment in place, shall we say.

62
00:04:31,114 --> 00:04:31,850
Scott: I think it'll be fine.

63
00:04:32,090 --> 00:04:34,315
Rym: Two, the controls are hard-coded.

64
00:04:34,716 --> 00:04:41,910
Rym: You better have a legitimate real Xbox controller or something that the computer thinks is an Xbox controller plugged in.

65
00:04:42,954 --> 00:04:43,523
Scott: We all have those.

66
00:04:44,191 --> 00:04:44,719
Rym: I don't anymore.

67
00:04:44,740 --> 00:04:45,370
Rym: I got rid of them all.

68
00:04:45,990 --> 00:04:47,550
Scott: You got rid of every gamepad you own.

69
00:04:47,710 --> 00:04:49,369
Rym: I only have Nintendo Pro controllers now.

70
00:04:49,692 --> 00:04:50,289
Scott: You can use that.

71
00:04:50,913 --> 00:04:51,728
Rym: Ah, no you can't.

72
00:04:52,695 --> 00:04:53,008
Scott: Why not?

73
00:04:53,291 --> 00:04:55,350
Rym: I don't think you know how the Pro controller works on Windows.

74
00:04:56,270 --> 00:04:57,750
Scott: It works just like every other controller.

75
00:04:57,830 --> 00:04:58,955
Rym: No, it actually doesn't.

76
00:04:59,035 --> 00:05:02,028
Rym: It has some special nonsense, and this is the problem.

77
00:05:03,151 --> 00:05:11,330
Rym: It works if the game has implemented it properly, or you run the game through Steam, because Steam has a layer that makes the Pro controller work perfectly.

78
00:05:11,693 --> 00:05:12,628
Rym: It'll just work.

79
00:05:13,710 --> 00:05:24,810
Rym: But because of the way this game was implemented, it wants the raw input from the computer, and the raw input from Nintendo Pro controller has a lot of fun stuff that happens.

80
00:05:25,371 --> 00:05:34,790
Rym: So, if you launch this game, and you have a Nintendo Pro controller plugged into your computer, this game will flip the fuck out and crash.

81
00:05:35,551 --> 00:05:38,906
Rym: If you unplug the controller, it'll work fine.

82
00:05:38,946 --> 00:05:39,609
Rym: I got rid of that too.

83
00:05:40,450 --> 00:05:41,930
Scott: You just threw all your controllers in the garbage?

84
00:05:42,050 --> 00:05:43,256
Rym: Yeah, the Pro controller's all I need.

85
00:05:43,276 --> 00:05:45,990
Rym: It works for every game I play on the computer except this janky-ass game.

86
00:05:46,250 --> 00:05:46,653
Scott: Apparently not.

87
00:05:46,673 --> 00:05:49,190
Scott: This is why I have a little horde of various controllers.

88
00:05:49,411 --> 00:05:50,274
Rym: Only this game.

89
00:05:50,616 --> 00:05:54,130
Rym: This game has hard-coded, non-changeable controls.

90
00:05:56,914 --> 00:06:04,010
Scott: Also, every other Windows PC game that, I guess according to you, if the Pro controller only works through Steam, every single game that's not on Steam, it might download.

91
00:06:04,170 --> 00:06:09,325
Rym: No, through Steam, or if the developer implemented controllers the good way instead of the raw way.

92
00:06:09,726 --> 00:06:11,230
Rym: I did a lot of research on this.

93
00:06:11,370 --> 00:06:18,770
Scott: So, the entire history of PC games before Steam and before the Nintendo Switch Pro controller, you now cannot use your gamepad with.

94
00:06:18,931 --> 00:06:21,089
Rym: This is the only game I have that is impacted by that.

95
00:06:21,890 --> 00:06:24,130
Scott: Yeah, well, you know, you're not playing old games.

96
00:06:24,631 --> 00:06:25,073
Rym: Yeah, no I'm not.

97
00:06:25,134 --> 00:06:27,950
Rym: And actually, old games, I've gotten to work just fine with that controller as well.

98
00:06:28,030 --> 00:06:37,270
Rym: But this game, if a troll controller is plugged, even if you don't use it, if you have an Xbox controller, but you happen to have a Pro controller plugged into your computer, this game will just fucking flip out and crash.

99
00:06:40,151 --> 00:06:46,930
Rym: So, the installer that this EXE launches does not fully work.

100
00:06:47,910 --> 00:06:57,981
Rym: It says it'll make shortcuts that it doesn't, among other issues, but it does install the game to a hard-coded location on your computer.

101
00:06:58,001 --> 00:07:06,290
Rym: that is in a place that implies the person who made this game has no idea how Windows works and barely understands how file systems work.

102
00:07:06,771 --> 00:07:07,373
Rym: I am shocked.

103
00:07:07,694 --> 00:07:11,744
Rym: this game launched and the installer was able to put those files where it put them.

104
00:07:12,326 --> 00:07:13,830
Rym: And this is all non-configurable.

105
00:07:15,390 --> 00:07:20,823
Rym: There's also a million buttons you can press on the keyboard that do insane stuff that you kind of got to ignore.

106
00:07:21,184 --> 00:07:24,010
Rym: Make sure you don't accidentally hit one of them while you're playing or the game will get weird.

107
00:07:24,551 --> 00:07:28,030
Rym: But if you get past all that jank, the game is absolutely perfect.

108
00:07:29,333 --> 00:07:29,689
Scott: Okay.

109
00:07:30,830 --> 00:07:32,993
Rym: So, I was not willing to get through that.

110
00:07:33,034 --> 00:07:34,856
Rym: I watched a long play, and good job.

111
00:07:35,097 --> 00:07:37,501
Rym: This person is hopefully going somewhere.

112
00:07:37,741 --> 00:07:43,270
Rym: The only thing they're bad at is programming an application that I would trust.

113
00:07:43,670 --> 00:07:49,644
Rym: In terms of the game design and the implementation of the game itself, inside of that window, if you get it to launch, perfect.

114
00:07:50,025 --> 00:07:52,370
Rym: Absolutely S-tier implementation.

115
00:07:54,711 --> 00:07:55,278
Rym: Has he got any news?

116
00:07:57,781 --> 00:07:58,783
Scott: I mean, I guess so, right?

117
00:07:58,863 --> 00:08:10,399
Scott: So, it's been known for a while now that Nintendo was going to be turning off all the internet biz for the Wii U and the 3DS.

118
00:08:12,402 --> 00:08:22,860
Scott: So, they already turned off all the Wii stuff, and now they turned off some eShop stuff, but now they're going to be turning off the ability to play games online.

119
00:08:23,160 --> 00:08:32,059
Scott: So, for example, if you want to play Smash Brothers for the Wii U over the internet against other people, you won't be able to do that anymore once they turn it off.

120
00:08:32,861 --> 00:08:38,056
Scott: And it's like, okay, how many people are still playing multiplayer online games for 3DS or Wii U?

121
00:08:39,400 --> 00:08:41,139
Rym: How many people can find a different game to play?

122
00:08:42,361 --> 00:08:49,220
Scott: Right, and it's like, even if we preserve that, if they kept it online, no one would be using it, right?

123
00:08:49,520 --> 00:09:01,740
Scott: I feel like people have already figured out a lot of those protocols, and there are ways that you can spin up, basically, a clone server, if we really need to get that experience somehow again.

124
00:09:03,640 --> 00:09:27,920
Scott: But, there is one key factor here that makes this devastating, is that Mario Maker 1 is a 3DS Wii U game, and even if you're not doing some real-time multiplayer action, which I don't even know if it might have that, I don't remember, but all the levels are stored on Nintendo's servers.

125
00:09:30,460 --> 00:09:47,920
Scott: So that is, if those servers are turned off, and you try to play Mario Maker 1, you'll just be able to make and play levels locally, and any levels you've downloaded, but you won't be able to access all kinds of levels from the internet.

126
00:09:48,040 --> 00:09:50,540
Scott: Even if you have the level code, you won't be able to get the level.

127
00:09:50,660 --> 00:09:55,139
Rym: But I'm sure Nintendo has backed up all these levels and will make them available on site.

128
00:09:56,741 --> 00:10:15,580
Scott: Right, so there is a treasure trove of perhaps millions, who knows how many, clearly many thousands, easily if not millions, of crafted levels for a game, many of which are garbage, but are no less worthy of being saved for all human eternity.

129
00:10:15,720 --> 00:10:25,400
Rym: Especially because the total amount of storage and effort that would be necessary to keep these perturbed and available, that rounds down to zero.

130
00:10:26,600 --> 00:10:43,480
Scott: Right, so that would be a damn shame if we lost all the Mario Maker levels, and there's also a lot of like, people have figured out a lot of the Mario Maker APIs for those servers, also for Mario Maker 2, so there's websites you can go to and type in level codes and it'll show you the level.

131
00:10:45,441 --> 00:10:59,440
Scott: But yeah, we need to like, you know, whoever's doing that needs to step up the efforts, and basically I think they realize this, and back the hell up every Mario Maker level as much as we possibly can, so they're not lost to time and space.

132
00:10:59,900 --> 00:11:43,179
Rym: See, if I were a dictator, one of the things I would do, but this is low on the list, like there's other things to do, but this is on the list somewhere, is there should be a requirement that if any service like this is made available and gets above a certain size, or exists long enough, like make up some criteria and have some experts decide where the line is, then if the company does not archive them and make them available, the government will archive them and make them available, and if the government is unwilling or unable to for whatever reason, then that triggers something where all copyright rules disappear and any random ass person who wants to archive this stuff can use whatever means necessary to do so.

133
00:11:46,303 --> 00:11:48,780
Rym: It's a shame when we lose culture and we lose stuff like this.

134
00:11:48,820 --> 00:11:51,525
Scott: It would be a real shame if we lost all the Mario Maker 1 levels.

135
00:11:51,585 --> 00:11:56,413
Scott: However, you know, what's happened so far is they're not gone yet.

136
00:11:56,894 --> 00:12:00,140
Scott: What's happened is that technology stuff, right?

137
00:12:01,120 --> 00:12:09,569
Scott: They clearly have many servers that are hosting all this stuff, but sometimes when you turn on Mario Maker, it doesn't work, and sometimes you turn it on and it does.

138
00:12:10,030 --> 00:12:20,120
Scott: Their load balancer must have all the servers hard coded, but it seems like they've turned off all the servers except one, so you have to keep hitting the load balancer until you get routed to the one working server.

139
00:12:20,260 --> 00:12:28,860
Rym: That pattern of failure describes roughly 20% of all the major outages I've ever had to deal with in my life as a technology professional.

140
00:12:30,702 --> 00:12:36,617
Scott: Yeah, and Nintendo doesn't seem to care about changing what the hard coded list and the load balancer, right?

141
00:12:36,657 --> 00:12:37,860
Scott: They just turned off the servers.

142
00:12:37,860 --> 00:12:40,340
Rym: Well, because they're shutting the whole thing down in like April anyway.

143
00:12:42,721 --> 00:12:42,881
Scott: Yeah.

144
00:12:42,941 --> 00:12:53,596
Scott: But anyway, someone's really going to back up Mario Maker 1, but we also have to realize in some number of years this is going to happen to Mario Maker 2, right?

145
00:12:53,696 --> 00:12:54,477
Scott: You have to assume.

146
00:12:54,497 --> 00:12:55,839
Scott: We'll see.

147
00:12:57,460 --> 00:13:05,100
Scott: But there are some hints that Nintendo's current network infrastructure might be maintained for the next console cycle.

148
00:13:05,422 --> 00:13:13,160
Scott: I could see that considering this is the first... The people who don't go crazy when all this money they spent on Switch games suddenly evaporates.

149
00:13:13,521 --> 00:13:16,186
Rym: I was thinking about this recently for unrelated reasons.

150
00:13:16,727 --> 00:13:23,480
Rym: This generation, unlike the Wii U, which just didn't penetrate that deeply, the Switch is so pervasive.

151
00:13:24,200 --> 00:13:30,092
Rym: And it came at a time when the entire world shifted to almost exclusively buying games digitally.

152
00:13:30,112 --> 00:13:31,535
Rym: That was one of those ESA reports.

153
00:13:31,855 --> 00:13:34,180
Rym: Nobody wants physical cartridges, even on Nintendo anymore.

154
00:13:34,821 --> 00:13:44,118
Rym: So now we're at a point where there's a critical mass where this current generation of Nintendo server and online service stuff might have to be the baseline.

155
00:13:44,158 --> 00:13:45,100
Rym: that continues forever.

156
00:13:45,280 --> 00:13:48,840
Rym: Like whatever account you got now, whatever stuff's in it will be available for a very long time.

157
00:13:48,860 --> 00:13:53,174
Scott: They definitely said they're going to keep your accounts, but it's still a question of will you keep all the games?

158
00:13:53,214 --> 00:13:55,060
Scott: Will all those games run in the next console?

159
00:13:55,220 --> 00:14:04,500
Scott: And then it's another thing to say, okay, your games run the next console, but will the server with all the Mario Maker 2 levels continue to be on and running for how long?

160
00:14:06,041 --> 00:14:11,220
Scott: For as long as, you know, just because the console plays Mario Maker 2 and Mario Maker 3 came out, whatever.

161
00:14:11,721 --> 00:14:19,020
Scott: But Mario Maker 2, if Mario Maker 1 is like a pirate's treasure, Mario Maker 2 is like Smaug's treasure.

162
00:14:19,522 --> 00:14:24,040
Scott: It's like, you know, talking about the levels that are in on those servers.

163
00:14:24,660 --> 00:14:31,420
Scott: So we got to make sure that that's not lost if Nintendo decides they don't want to maintain those servers anymore.

164
00:14:31,780 --> 00:14:37,020
Rym: It's almost like we just did a panel talking about this exact topic at PAX West.

165
00:14:37,820 --> 00:14:42,760
Scott: I'm just saying it's, you know, it's a unique game in terms of games, period.

166
00:14:43,120 --> 00:14:44,140
Rym: I think it's an important game.

167
00:14:45,021 --> 00:14:52,180
Scott: It's a super important game that does not get... Basically, it's like there's people who pay a bunch of attention to it, and then there's people who pay no attention.

168
00:14:54,441 --> 00:15:03,080
Rym: So in some other news, this is... I'm going to start by saying that I approve of everything that happened here, and I wish more companies did this more aggressively.

169
00:15:04,140 --> 00:15:18,100
Rym: Valve did a thing with Dota 2, a game I don't play, where for Christmas, or as they call it, Frostavis, they did a thing where all the players are going to get a little gift-wrapped item, and when they open it, they get some special cosmetics or some stuff in the game.

170
00:15:18,940 --> 00:15:31,960
Rym: But, rolled into that gift, anyone that they had added to their "To Be Banned" list, who was not banned yet, they delivered all of the bans via this gift.

171
00:15:32,540 --> 00:15:51,140
Rym: So some many, many tens of thousands of people, based on this reporting, we don't have a specific number that they have announced, at least as far as I've found, when they opened their gift box expecting to get a skin, they got a lump of toxic coal, and then their account shut down and they were banned permanently.

172
00:15:51,701 --> 00:15:59,140
Rym: And this happened to at least one moderately popular live streamer, like live on stream, which is just icing on the cake.

173
00:15:59,880 --> 00:16:10,500
Rym: Now, the bans in particular here are interesting, because this was not bans related to cheating and other things, this was two specific kinds of bans.

174
00:16:11,142 --> 00:16:22,860
Rym: It was people who were sharing accounts between high-skill and low-skill players, like a pro player sharing an account with someone who was not in fact the pro, and it was people who were, by Valve's estimation, smurfing.

175
00:16:23,520 --> 00:16:32,000
Rym: They went after people who were causing quality of life issues for other players in the matchmaking, and just banning them en masse.

176
00:16:33,682 --> 00:16:56,187
Scott: I mean, these kinds of games always have people who are behaving poorly, because that's how they're designed to encourage that kind of behavior, and they have to do manual and some automatic moderation of people, but the cleverness of someone thinks they're opening a loot chest and it's like, "Haha, you're banned,".

177
00:16:56,288 --> 00:16:57,459
Scott: and they actually did that.

178
00:16:59,461 --> 00:17:07,680
Rym: That's the kind of thing that a lot of companies, I feel like they're afraid of doing, but one, I don't think they suffered any negative PR, because everyone who thinks this is funny- No, it's all positive.

179
00:17:07,839 --> 00:17:11,060
Rym: Yeah, everyone who thinks this is funny hates the kind of people who got banned.

180
00:17:11,339 --> 00:17:15,298
Rym: Those are the people who ruined games for you, and meanwhile, the people who got banned, fuck them.

181
