373
00:31:28,197 --> 00:31:38,050
Scott: Um, but many people are anti-con, I know, I know people who are my friends who will refuse, they will not play absolutely no matter what game it is, even if it's their favorite game in the world.

374
00:31:38,591 --> 00:31:47,430
Scott: If it's online against any, even one human, they don't know, even if that other human can't talk to them or communicate in any way, they won't play that game.

375
00:31:47,570 --> 00:31:49,212
Rym: They just refuse, right?

376
00:31:49,572 --> 00:31:51,214
Scott: So here you go.

377
00:31:51,354 --> 00:32:01,225
Scott: Here's a game you only play with your friends against the environment only, uh, and has humorous aspects and is relatively accessible.

378
00:32:01,425 --> 00:32:05,169
Scott: and failure isn't punishing because failure is hilarious, right?

379
00:32:05,249 --> 00:32:05,950
Scott: Losing is fun.

380
00:32:06,293 --> 00:32:07,930
Rym: That's an important aspect.

381
00:32:08,211 --> 00:32:08,830
Scott: At least sometimes.

382
00:32:09,750 --> 00:32:22,410
Rym: Yeah, well, that's where I want to say, cause I want to start with the positive first because this game, to be clear and fair, has deep and possibly irredeemable flaws.

383
00:32:23,171 --> 00:32:26,978
Scott: I think it, I think it's, I don't think it's any of it's irredeemable.

384
00:32:27,078 --> 00:32:33,910
Scott: I think it's just, you would have to polish this for like a long ass time to get to fix the flaws.

385
00:32:35,690 --> 00:32:43,550
Rym: I, to do what this game is doing justice, it would have to be re implemented from scratch, but maybe, I think so.

386
00:32:44,030 --> 00:32:53,150
Rym: But anyway, I want to focus on the positive first because while this game has a lot of jank and a lot of problems, it's done a few things like shockingly well.

387
00:32:54,055 --> 00:32:56,770
Rym: And there's a reason why it's getting so much buzz.

388
00:32:57,170 --> 00:33:16,050
Rym: The, if I have to, the game loop that has been presented here is this perfect blend of absurdity, paranoia, the RPG, like style humor, uh, rules not being explained to you and it's actually better and funnier that you don't know how it works.

389
00:33:16,591 --> 00:33:30,150
Rym: Like the game loop is just really compelling and fun coupled with, this is the first game I have ever played where proximity audio works really well, is fun, is necessary, like this is the model.

390
00:33:30,511 --> 00:33:35,490
Rym: Any game you ever make that does proximity audio, fucking do it the way they did it here, uh, and whatever.

391
00:33:35,670 --> 00:33:37,276
Scott: I don't know why more games haven't done it.

392
00:33:37,296 --> 00:33:41,190
Scott: It's taken this long and we've been talking about it since like, you know, the year 2000.

393
00:33:41,190 --> 00:33:42,555
Scott: No game has done it well.

394
00:33:42,655 --> 00:33:43,578
Rym: It always sucks.

395
00:33:43,678 --> 00:33:46,086
Rym: And yet this game has it perfect.

396
00:33:46,186 --> 00:33:47,370
Rym: It is so good.

397
00:33:48,030 --> 00:33:52,730
Scott: If the people who made this game can do it, the people who spend millions of dollars on a game can do it, right?

398
00:33:53,270 --> 00:33:53,410
Rym: Exactly.

399
00:33:53,491 --> 00:33:54,312
Rym: But yet they don't.

400
00:33:54,393 --> 00:33:58,282
Rym: I've never, ever, even VR chat barely gets it to work.

401
00:33:58,302 --> 00:34:00,467
Rym: And that's not even a, that's not a game in this sense.

402
00:34:00,487 --> 00:34:01,470
Rym: That's a different kind of game.

403
00:34:02,171 --> 00:34:06,000
Rym: But, uh, so let's talk about what's good about this first, this game, the way it works.

404
00:34:06,963 --> 00:34:10,010
Rym: Uh, there's no documentation unless you read a Wiki.

405
00:34:10,650 --> 00:34:13,810
Scott: There's a tutorial in the game that gets you enough information.

406
00:34:14,010 --> 00:34:14,130
Scott: Yep.

407
00:34:14,672 --> 00:34:16,797
Rym: And you kind of just roll with it.

408
00:34:16,837 --> 00:34:22,389
Rym: You're a bunch of dipshits in a corporate dystopia on a dipshitty ship that sucks.

409
00:34:23,051 --> 00:34:26,056
Rym: And there's a computer you can type into to figure out what's going on.

410
00:34:26,496 --> 00:34:33,429
Rym: You basically just got to go planet to planet where various failed industrial projects have happened.

411
00:34:34,130 --> 00:34:41,406
Rym: And find as much garbage as you can to sell to mysterious aliens so they don't kill you and you can slowly upgrade your shit.

412
00:34:41,907 --> 00:34:43,429
Rym: And that's kind of the whole game.

413
00:34:44,331 --> 00:34:46,313
Scott: And it's basically, it's a roguelike, right?

414
00:34:46,373 --> 00:34:51,762
Scott: It's like you go out with your team and you go to a planet, you have to meet quota, right?

415
00:34:51,862 --> 00:34:56,590
Scott: And then you go back and sell, you go out again, you meet quota, you sell the quota keeps going up.

416
00:34:57,110 --> 00:34:58,953
Scott: And then when you fail, you're back to square one.

417
00:34:59,353 --> 00:35:14,573
Scott: So I don't know if there's some sort of end game, like you've reached the highest quota and there's a congratulations you completed, but it's like, as long as you don't completely fuck up and everyone dies and it's the end, or you don't fail to meet quota,

418
00:35:14,593 --> 00:35:15,274
Rym: you

419
00:35:15,675 --> 00:35:24,086
Scott: just keep going and you carry all the stuff that you had, you still have all your equipment that you didn't lose, all the, you know, everything, right?

420
00:35:24,527 --> 00:35:26,590
Scott: All the upgrades you've made to your ship or whatever.

421
00:35:27,111 --> 00:35:33,008
Scott: It just carries forward and it's stateful in your save game until you fail, then that's it.

422
00:35:33,088 --> 00:35:33,590
Scott: It's all gone.

423
00:35:34,191 --> 00:35:49,490
Rym: And it's a very good example of a certain kind of game design and gameplay that centers around, you'll encounter a thing, whatever it is, could be a mechanic, could be an object in a game, like whatever it is, and the game doesn't tell you how it works.

424
00:35:49,910 --> 00:35:52,074
Rym: And you kind of just got to poke at it and see how it goes.

425
00:35:52,475 --> 00:35:55,702
Rym: And that discovery aspect is really fun.

426
00:35:56,143 --> 00:35:57,345
Rym: Like this game does it well.

427
00:35:57,806 --> 00:35:59,430
Rym: Like there's landmines, spoilers, I guess.

428
00:35:59,951 --> 00:36:03,580
Rym: At one point we see a thing on the ground and it's got a little red light and it beeps.

429
00:36:03,640 --> 00:36:04,562
Rym: if any way it's close to it.

430
00:36:05,124 --> 00:36:07,008
Rym: Now the game has not told us what the fuck this is.

431
00:36:07,028 --> 00:36:07,770
Rym: We're just going to look at it.

432
00:36:08,271 --> 00:36:17,065
Scott: You have a scanner that basically this scanner, you're jamming the button for the scanner all the time because it identifies all the interactable objects within distance.

433
00:36:18,427 --> 00:36:20,250
Scott: And it'll tell you that's a landmine.

434
00:36:20,310 --> 00:36:20,510
Scott: Yep.

435
00:36:20,530 --> 00:36:23,597
Rym: So the scanner's like landmine, but all right, so how do I interact with this?

436
00:36:23,637 --> 00:36:26,222
Rym: Can I, how close can I get to it before it blows up?

437
00:36:26,543 --> 00:36:27,845
Rym: There's only one way to find out.

438
00:36:27,925 --> 00:36:29,649
Rym: And it usually involves someone blowing up.

439
00:36:31,011 --> 00:36:40,807
Scott: I mean, you know, and it's like you only have so many items and you can buy items and it's not always clear like, you know, what an item does.

440
00:36:40,847 --> 00:36:42,290
Scott: Sometimes it is, sometimes it isn't.

441
00:36:42,770 --> 00:36:48,070
Rym: And some of the items are mostly worthless or dangerous or bad, which is even funnier.

442
00:36:48,250 --> 00:36:59,530
Scott: It's not, and it's not clear like what items interact with which things, I guess you can look it up or you can experiment with every possible item, but the items cost money, which makes it hard to reach quota if you buy too many.

443
00:37:00,131 --> 00:37:11,110
Scott: So like I saw these, like there's these beehives lying around and it's like, I kind of want to bring the beehives back and sell them cause I heard that they're valuable, but there's bees that kill you if you get too close.

444
00:37:11,631 --> 00:37:13,315
Scott: And I'm like, how do I get rid of the bees?

445
00:37:13,816 --> 00:37:19,327
Scott: And it's like maybe one person can distract the bees cause you can run away from them while another person steals the hive.

446
00:37:19,848 --> 00:37:20,750
Scott: You know, that could work.

447
00:37:21,471 --> 00:37:22,613
Scott: I tried to dynamite them.

448
00:37:22,914 --> 00:37:23,795
Scott: I didn't do anything.

449
00:37:24,176 --> 00:37:27,884
Rym: It's like, I hit the hive with the shovel and that just killed me.

450
00:37:28,605 --> 00:37:29,667
Scott: Yes, that would kill you.

451
00:37:29,747 --> 00:37:31,030
Scott: Yes, that's a bad idea.

452
00:37:31,130 --> 00:37:32,051
Scott: That was a very bad idea.

453
00:37:32,252 --> 00:37:35,116
Rym: Or like I bought a jet pack cause I was like, I want to see what this jet pack does.

454
00:37:35,576 --> 00:37:37,178
Rym: And it has really complex controls.

455
00:37:37,218 --> 00:37:39,161
Rym: Like it's a, it's not what you expect.

456
00:37:39,582 --> 00:37:44,829
Rym: So I try to use it and I fly up a little bit, immediately tumble forward and immediately crash the wall and die.

457
00:37:44,869 --> 00:37:45,189
Rym: The end.

458
00:37:45,470 --> 00:37:45,650
Rym: Dead.

459
00:37:46,350 --> 00:37:46,450
Scott: Yeah.

460
00:37:46,470 --> 00:37:48,780
Scott: I don't, I don't know what the jet pack would be useful for.

461
00:37:48,820 --> 00:37:51,270
Rym: I couldn't find any situation where that jet pack was useful.

462
00:37:52,151 --> 00:37:52,311
Scott: Yeah.

463
00:37:52,632 --> 00:38:00,562
Scott: But basically any planet you land on, there's the place you land and there's a little overworld and there's some stuff in the overworld, but not much.

464
00:38:00,763 --> 00:38:06,010
Scott: Usually there's just an entrance to the real game area that you basically go over to.

465
00:38:06,690 --> 00:38:12,216
Scott: And once you go inside there, it's some sort of like dungeon, warehousey kind of place.

466
00:38:13,016 --> 00:38:18,461
Scott: Um, maze like thing that's, that's procedurally generated with a random seed every time.

467
00:38:18,481 --> 00:38:21,324
Scott: And there's hazards in it.

468
00:38:21,364 --> 00:38:25,688
Scott: There's treasures, there's objects, there's doors that you can unlock.

469
00:38:25,708 --> 00:38:27,870
Scott: There's all kinds of stuff going on in there.

470
00:38:28,614 --> 00:38:31,049
Rym: There's also often monsters and problems.

471
00:38:31,670 --> 00:38:31,770
Scott: Yeah.

472
00:38:31,790 --> 00:38:38,290
Scott: And you're trying to get as much of the stuff out of there that, and bring it back to your ship as you can without dying.

473
00:38:40,314 --> 00:38:41,388
Scott: And that's, that's that.

474
00:38:42,170 --> 00:38:49,763
Rym: But the, the biggest key, like the, the most important lesson from this game is this proximity audio, because when you're all in the ship, you're just talking.

475
00:38:50,083 --> 00:38:52,107
Rym: Like the voice comms in the game work really well.

476
00:38:52,147 --> 00:38:53,209
Rym: They're like, they're the right volume.

477
00:38:53,249 --> 00:38:53,890
Rym: Everything just works.

478
00:38:54,552 --> 00:39:01,710
Rym: When you start the game, the first thing it yells at you about is make sure your mic works because the game is all about the proximity audio.

479
00:39:02,410 --> 00:39:04,970
Scott: Now you could cheat and use a separate audio communicator.

480
00:39:05,170 --> 00:39:05,877
Scott: That would ruin the game.

481
00:39:06,321 --> 00:39:07,129
Rym: Absolutely ruin it.

482
00:39:07,570 --> 00:39:08,251
Scott: It's like that.

483
00:39:08,392 --> 00:39:11,398
Scott: Sure that I guess, but that's, you're just cheating yourselves.

484
00:39:11,538 --> 00:39:14,524
Scott: because now it's like, okay, congrats.

485
00:39:14,564 --> 00:39:17,470
Scott: You, you, you defeated the purpose of the game.

486
00:39:17,650 --> 00:39:17,730
Rym: Yup.

487
00:39:17,750 --> 00:39:21,303
Rym: It's like, it's like opening your eyes playing mafia or werewolf at a table.

488
00:39:21,343 --> 00:39:23,130
Rym: Like why the fuck you even playing at that point?

489
00:39:23,631 --> 00:39:24,192
Scott: Exactly.

490
00:39:24,773 --> 00:39:30,043
Rym: So I guess I'll describe a few things that happened early on and you'll see like how well this works.

491
00:39:30,103 --> 00:39:33,690
Rym: Cause I haven't like interrogated the specifics of like the fall off.

492
00:39:33,790 --> 00:39:41,765
Rym: But basically we all go into the place and our friend, other Scott says something like, Oh, Hey, I think I see something moving down there.

493
00:39:41,846 --> 00:39:43,649
Rym: And he's getting quieter as he's saying it.

494
00:39:44,210 --> 00:39:45,793
Rym: I just never hear anything from him again.

495
00:39:46,755 --> 00:39:52,867
Rym: And then we go back to the ship and we're ready to take off and all of us are in the ship and he hasn't come back and we see he hasn't moved on the monitor.

496
00:39:52,927 --> 00:39:53,549
Rym: So we're like, fuck it.

497
00:39:53,569 --> 00:39:54,110
Rym: And we just leave.

498
00:39:54,750 --> 00:39:58,497
Rym: And thus we learn that one, when someone gets too far away, you just can't hear them.

499
00:39:58,978 --> 00:40:05,890
Rym: And two, when you die, you enter a voice chat of only the other people who are dead.

500
00:40:06,030 --> 00:40:08,392
Rym: So if you're the only one dead, you're just there by yourself.

501
00:40:09,033 --> 00:40:26,910
Rym: But when a second person dies, like for example, when there was a blob of death slime hanging out right on the other side of a door that had killed two of us, the two of you who were dead were basically laughing about how I can't wait until Rym and other Scott try to open this door and both die.

502
00:40:27,350 --> 00:40:31,916
Rym: And sure enough, eventually I walk over to this door and like, I wonder what they're doing in there.

503
00:40:31,956 --> 00:40:32,817
Rym: They can't be dead.

504
00:40:32,837 --> 00:40:34,879
Rym: And I open the door and I immediately die.

505
00:40:34,899 --> 00:40:44,310
Rym: And the first thing I hear is you two laughing your asses off at me because you've listened to me and watched me walk toward my death in real time.

506
00:40:45,111 --> 00:40:45,271
Scott: Yeah.

507
00:40:45,291 --> 00:40:49,035
Scott: There's a lot of fun spectating the people who are still alive when you're dead.

508
00:40:49,996 --> 00:41:00,388
Scott: Also, you know, somebody can and maybe should stay on the ship and not go try to get loot because there's a computer on the ship and security cameras.

509
00:41:00,408 --> 00:41:02,110
Scott: They can sort of monitor the situation.

510
00:41:03,011 --> 00:41:13,422
Scott: But if you can buy walkie talkies, you can communicate with people and like open doors to them and do all kinds of stuff from the ship.

511
00:41:13,863 --> 00:41:20,430
Scott: But when you're on the ship, there's like situations where like monsters will come and eat you while you're in the ship.

512
00:41:21,131 --> 00:41:21,952
Scott: And you can.

513
00:41:22,152 --> 00:41:28,981
Scott: it's a little bit five nights at Freddy's where like you can push a button to close the door, but the door on the ship only stays closed for a little bit.

514
00:41:29,542 --> 00:41:33,427
Scott: And eventually you either you could jam the button again, but then the must.

515
00:41:33,467 --> 00:41:34,628
Scott: you might not have enough time.

516
00:41:34,708 --> 00:41:35,730
Scott: The monster might come get you.

517
00:41:36,251 --> 00:41:39,878
Scott: So your only other option at that point is to take off and leave everyone behind.

518
00:41:39,898 --> 00:41:40,840
Scott: And but you left.

519
00:41:40,880 --> 00:41:42,062
Scott: you took off with no treasure.

520
00:41:42,523 --> 00:41:45,670
Scott: So you're not going to make quota anyway if you do that.

521
00:41:46,410 --> 00:41:49,896
Rym: I remember thinking Alex hasn't said anything on the walkie talkie anymore.

522
00:41:49,936 --> 00:41:50,557
Rym: I wonder how like.

523
00:41:50,597 --> 00:41:51,718
Rym: I wonder what that's about.

524
00:41:51,759 --> 00:41:58,950
Rym: Did he come into the thing and I come toward the ship and I see this like monster standing like right at the entrance to the ship moving around.

525
00:41:59,050 --> 00:42:00,073
Rym: I'm like, oh, no, what's that?

526
00:42:00,434 --> 00:42:02,278
Rym: And then it sees me and it runs at me and kills me.

527
00:42:02,299 --> 00:42:04,745
Rym: Then Alex is like, yeah, it killed me.

528
00:42:04,845 --> 00:42:06,610
Rym: Like as soon as you guys left.

529
00:42:07,171 --> 00:42:07,351
Scott: Yeah.

530
00:42:07,371 --> 00:42:15,126
Scott: It's like there might be an item that could deal with these things, but it's like you won't really have a lot of chances to like experiment and figure it out.

531
00:42:15,146 --> 00:42:15,306
Scott: Right.

532
00:42:15,346 --> 00:42:17,250
Scott: Which one is going to deal with which.

533
00:42:17,270 --> 00:42:17,490
Scott: Right.

534
00:42:18,331 --> 00:42:21,016
Scott: And there's a lot of things that kill you super quickly.

535
00:42:21,136 --> 00:42:28,970
Scott: And it's like the only way that you could have lived is if someone else was nearby with the right item and the knowledge to use that item and the knowledge of what was happening.

536
00:42:29,611 --> 00:42:37,419
Scott: Um, so if you play the game a lot, I'm sure over time you could learn all the monsters and hazards and learn the right ways to deal with them.

537
00:42:37,939 --> 00:42:39,441
Scott: But that'll take quite a lot of play.

538
00:42:39,481 --> 00:42:47,930
Scott: We had a lot of success just going in, getting safe to get loot, not trying to go to go through hazards to get greedy.

539
00:42:48,490 --> 00:42:52,675
Rym: If we didn't see or hear from someone for more than like a minute, we just assumed they were dead.

540
00:42:52,836 --> 00:42:56,079
Scott: And just playing it safe and just grabbing loot.

541
00:42:56,119 --> 00:42:59,724
Scott: that wasn't difficult to get, you know, we were able to make.

542
00:42:59,984 --> 00:43:04,189
Scott: eventually once we figured it out, we were able to make quota like several times in a row.

543
00:43:04,209 --> 00:43:04,870
Scott: Right.

544
00:43:05,531 --> 00:43:19,229
Scott: Um, yeah, we got better, really upgrade, upgrade our equipment and upgrade our, you know, stuff and keep going without having to, you know, just by playing it safe and conservatively not messing around, not taking big risks, you know, see a slime.

545
00:43:19,489 --> 00:43:20,150
Scott: Don't go that way.

546
00:43:20,570 --> 00:43:21,351
Scott: Just don't go that way.

547
00:43:21,632 --> 00:43:22,273
Scott: That's that simple.

548
00:43:22,513 --> 00:43:22,713
Scott: Right.

549
00:43:23,094 --> 00:43:24,897
Scott: We don't got to figure out what beats slime yet.

550
00:43:25,157 --> 00:43:25,338
Scott: Right.

551
00:43:25,398 --> 00:43:26,259
Scott: Let's just not go there.

552
00:43:26,880 --> 00:43:33,310
Scott: And then I'm sure over time, though, if you keep doing that, the conservative play, you'll have so much money and so much equipment.

553
00:43:33,910 --> 00:43:41,577
Scott: Then you'll have easier opportunities at trying to, you know, dig for more and you'll have to dig for more because the quota will go up.

554
00:43:41,617 --> 00:43:41,957
Scott: Yeah.

555
00:43:42,818 --> 00:43:47,161
Rym: And I guess this game, the game loop is really satisfying.

556
00:43:47,181 --> 00:43:47,622
Rym: There's a lot like.

557
00:43:47,662 --> 00:43:57,470
Rym: I don't want to tell a bunch of stories because half the fun is encountering various ridiculous situations in your first or second playthrough.

558
00:43:58,251 --> 00:44:04,684
Rym: But that said, the game as it is now, as of when we recorded this episode, was about four hours worth of fun.

559
00:44:04,964 --> 00:44:07,650
Rym: And I don't feel the need to revisit it in its current state.

560
00:44:08,610 --> 00:44:13,399
Scott: No, I think that, you know, it's basically we didn't, you know, we made quota a bunch of times.

561
00:44:13,819 --> 00:44:19,970
Scott: I'm sure there are things I haven't seen there, you know, but there are planets we didn't go to because it costs too much money to even go to.

562
00:44:20,030 --> 00:44:23,355
Rym: I read a wiki and learned a lot about the monsters and the things we didn't see.

563
00:44:23,395 --> 00:44:24,838
Rym: And I have some things I can say about that.

564
00:44:24,878 --> 00:44:25,358
Rym: There's some.

565
00:44:25,819 --> 00:44:32,770
Rym: there's some game design around all those aspects of it compared to the core loop, which is phenomenal and the voice, which is phenomenal.

566
00:44:33,510 --> 00:44:33,731
Scott: Right.

567
00:44:33,791 --> 00:44:37,937
Scott: But it's like, yeah, I, you know, I got sort of what I needed to get out of the game.

568
00:44:38,418 --> 00:44:46,430
Scott: But because it's so unpolished, it's like I didn't feel like there was any more to like just continuing to play anymore.

569
00:44:46,570 --> 00:44:50,477
Scott: It's like it's like, you know, the fun was like, OK, this is sort of the same.

570
00:44:50,497 --> 00:44:51,319
Scott: We figured this out.

571
00:44:51,740 --> 00:44:53,403
Scott: What we found out was a good thing.

572
00:44:53,423 --> 00:44:55,767
Scott: And I had my fill of that good thing.

573
00:44:55,787 --> 00:44:57,490
Scott: And now I don't need any more.

574
00:44:57,910 --> 00:44:58,812
Scott: I'm upset.

575
00:44:59,593 --> 00:45:06,243
Scott: unless you really upgrade this game a lot and add lots of new content or put tons of more stuff into it.

576
00:45:06,704 --> 00:45:08,306
Scott: There's no reason for me to play again.

577
00:45:08,327 --> 00:45:10,550
Scott: But I'm not upset with what I did already.

578
00:45:10,790 --> 00:45:11,051
Scott: Right.

579
00:45:11,431 --> 00:45:13,534
Scott: The amount of money and time that I spent.

580
00:45:14,255 --> 00:45:14,415
Scott: Yeah.

581
00:45:14,896 --> 00:45:16,578
Scott: It's like I paid whatever money.

582
00:45:16,778 --> 00:45:17,559
Scott: I saw a movie.

583
00:45:17,900 --> 00:45:18,821
Scott: The movie was done.

584
00:45:18,841 --> 00:45:20,183
Scott: I like the movie.

585
00:45:20,583 --> 00:45:22,065
Scott: Would I recommend someone else see it?

586
00:45:22,205 --> 00:45:22,466
Scott: Yes.

587
00:45:22,706 --> 00:45:23,507
Scott: But I watch it again.

588
00:45:23,527 --> 00:45:25,790
Rym: No, this game costs less.

589
00:45:26,210 --> 00:45:27,052
Rym: Yeah, it's ten bucks.

590
00:45:27,493 --> 00:45:30,599
Rym: I pay more than that to see a movie for 90 minutes in New York City.

591
00:45:31,121 --> 00:45:32,904
Scott: And this is a four hour game, basically.

592
00:45:33,806 --> 00:45:35,530
Scott: So a whole evening with friends.

593
00:45:36,191 --> 00:45:43,204
Rym: So to warn you, though, like what is wrong with this game is one, the developer did a good job like this game loop.

594
00:45:43,264 --> 00:45:44,686
Rym: There is some magic here.

595
00:45:44,866 --> 00:45:46,830
Rym: Like there's a lot of very good game design.

596
00:45:47,191 --> 00:45:49,094
Rym: But the developer, I think this appears there.

597
00:45:49,174 --> 00:45:50,756
Rym: This is their first PC game.

598
00:45:50,776 --> 00:45:52,720
Rym: They were previously a Roblox game developer.

599
00:45:52,740 --> 00:45:59,050
Rym: I think we're going to see a lot of people entering the industry who primarily made games and Roblox the way kids are.

600
00:45:59,791 --> 00:45:59,991
Scott: Good.

601
00:46:00,291 --> 00:46:00,492
Rym: Yep.

602
00:46:00,772 --> 00:46:01,693
Rym: I'm all for it.

603
00:46:02,354 --> 00:46:15,430
Rym: But as a result, it brings with it a number of specific kinds of game design tropes that exist in that Roblox world that you don't really interact with in more well, like professionally designed games yet.

604
00:46:16,074 --> 00:46:18,550
Scott: I've never played a Roblox, so I don't know what church in the Roblox world.

605
00:46:18,590 --> 00:46:21,273
Rym: I read a bunch about this to understand.

606
00:46:22,253 --> 00:46:39,850
Rym: The monsters, the things in the game that move around like the spiders and that weird jack in the box thing, they're very Roblox-y, according to a lot of people, in that the monsters behave the way a lot of Roblox games behave, where they're all super weird and have a super specific trick.

607
00:46:40,451 --> 00:46:49,530
Rym: And the game around the monsters is all about recognizing them, learning the very specific trick, and then developing a series of ways to overcome them.

608
00:46:49,650 --> 00:46:55,498
Rym: But they're hyper specific, and a lot of the monsters are really weird and unsatisfying.

609
00:46:56,640 --> 00:47:03,870
Rym: They tend to follow a certain kind of loop, hunt players in a very particular way, always behave exactly the same way every time.

610
00:47:04,553 --> 00:47:07,404
Rym: Once you know how to deal with a monster, it'll never be a problem again for you.

611
00:47:07,465 --> 00:47:08,690
Rym: It's that kind of loop.

612
00:47:09,810 --> 00:47:12,075
Rym: And the monsters were not satisfying.

613
00:47:12,215 --> 00:47:18,790
Rym: They were all kind of just janky and broken, and the hitboxes didn't work, because the game's very early access and very beta.

614
00:47:20,271 --> 00:47:25,449
Rym: Sometimes a monster would attack you and just not kill you because you were slightly higher than it and the hitbox misses you.

615
00:47:25,971 --> 00:47:32,450
Rym: Or sometimes you can't see a monster, but it kills you because it was clipping through the wall in a way that you could never perceive.

616
00:47:33,052 --> 00:47:36,504
Rym: The monsters could use a game design refactor, I think.

617
00:47:36,805 --> 00:47:38,150
Rym: They don't really have interesting behaviors.

618
00:47:38,270 --> 00:47:42,644
Rym: But again, early access, this is like the most unpolished version of this game.

619
00:47:43,386 --> 00:47:44,670
Rym: All these things could be changed.

620
00:47:45,451 --> 00:47:54,570
Scott: I mean, the thing is, if you look at the history of PC gaming as a whole, and video games in general, there are people who invented basically new kinds of games, right?

621
00:47:55,152 --> 00:48:00,970
Scott: And it's like there came Wolfenstein 3D Doom, and then suddenly everyone was making FPSs, right?

622
00:48:01,290 --> 00:48:05,430
Scott: There came the Warcraft and the Command and Conquer, and everyone was making RTSs suddenly, right?

623
00:48:05,750 --> 00:48:08,040
Scott: And then a lot of these came from mods, right?

624
00:48:08,080 --> 00:48:10,530
Scott: People made mods for games.

625
00:48:11,754 --> 00:48:13,210
Scott: Like Counter Strike was just a mob.

626
00:48:13,990 --> 00:48:19,130
Scott: Yeah, PUBG was just an ARMA mod, and then suddenly there were battle royales everywhere, right?

627
00:48:19,411 --> 00:48:25,150
Scott: You know, Dota was just a mob for Warcraft 3, and then there were MOBAs everywhere.

628
00:48:26,231 --> 00:48:33,390
Scott: So, you know, it seems like there was a period of time where there weren't so many new types of games.

629
00:48:33,650 --> 00:48:41,250
Scott: But in the recent years, we've had sort of the auto battlers, like the auto chess type.

630
00:48:41,571 --> 00:48:45,210
Scott: We've had the Vampire Survivors kind of game.

631
00:48:45,471 --> 00:48:48,650
Scott: We've had the Slay the Spire kind of card roguelike game.

632
00:48:49,591 --> 00:48:55,930
Scott: And all of those have spawned many, many clones and copycats of various shapes and sizes.

633
00:48:55,930 --> 00:48:57,735
Rym: I am confident this one will, too.

634
00:48:57,936 --> 00:49:02,850
Rym: I imagine there are like ten different designers all re-implementing this core game loop right now.

635
00:49:03,672 --> 00:49:07,670
Scott: And more than a lot of those other ones, you know, the original was like the good one.

636
00:49:07,850 --> 00:49:10,748
Scott: Like Dominion is a board game, but it's the same where it's like...

637
00:49:10,990 --> 00:49:12,530
Rym: Dominion's still the best deck builder.

638
00:49:13,310 --> 00:49:17,590
Scott: Yeah, as a deck builder goes, hard to beat Dominion, even though it was like the first one, basically.

639
00:49:17,670 --> 00:49:20,350
Rym: This might still be the best worker placement game now that I've revisited it.

640
00:49:20,410 --> 00:49:24,902
Scott: I don't know about that, but the point is, is that this game is, you know, it's not as original an idea.

641
00:49:28,351 --> 00:49:30,844
Scott: It's still just basically a Left 4 Dead PvE.

642
00:49:30,905 --> 00:49:31,790
Scott: It's not that unique.

643
00:49:32,570 --> 00:49:36,770
Scott: But it's still, you know, there isn't, I can't think of another game that is exactly that.

644
00:49:36,890 --> 00:49:40,150
Scott: Maybe sort of the, what's the dwarf mining one?

645
00:49:43,956 --> 00:49:44,530
Scott: What's it called?

646
00:49:45,190 --> 00:49:46,130
Rym: Which dwarf mining one?

647
00:49:46,170 --> 00:49:47,609
Scott: I played it when it was free on Steam.

648
00:49:48,350 --> 00:49:49,690
Scott: The space dwarfs who do the mining.

649
00:49:50,350 --> 00:49:52,029
Rym: I don't think I ever, I don't think I ever played that one.

650
00:49:54,935 --> 00:49:55,590
Scott: Well, anyway.

651
00:49:55,690 --> 00:49:56,829
Rym: I don't know what game you're talking about.

652
00:49:58,290 --> 00:50:05,090
Rym: But I guess, yeah, this, I have not seen this particular loop implemented, like this kind of game.

653
00:50:05,730 --> 00:50:08,550
Rym: This feels like it's gonna start one of those...

654
00:50:08,610 --> 00:50:09,950
Scott: Deep Rock Galactic, that one.

655
00:50:10,131 --> 00:50:11,089
Rym: Oh yeah, I never played that.

656
00:50:11,712 --> 00:50:17,090
Scott: Yeah, that's basically, it's similar to where it's PvE co-op deal, right?

657
00:50:18,790 --> 00:50:24,330
Scott: But, you know, it's, this is significantly, I guess, different than Deep Rock Galactic.

658
00:50:24,590 --> 00:50:28,090
Scott: Deep Rock Galactic is a little bit grindy in the way it goes.

659
00:50:28,230 --> 00:50:31,009
Scott: It doesn't have the roguelike element to it so much.

660
00:50:31,290 --> 00:50:32,310
Rym: Now that is the other trouble.

661
00:50:32,490 --> 00:50:35,230
Rym: The game, every, there's only a very small number of maps.

662
00:50:35,772 --> 00:50:41,070
Rym: And the procedurally generated content is pretty much just inside of the one building on each map.

663
00:50:42,072 --> 00:50:46,270
Rym: And it is not broad procedural generation.

664
00:50:46,811 --> 00:50:50,830
Rym: The layout is almost identical for every build, every kind of building.

665
00:50:51,671 --> 00:50:56,550
Rym: And 90% of all the places you can go are empty dead ends with nothing.

666
00:50:59,890 --> 00:51:08,170
Scott: So, yeah, I think somebody, this is an opportunity where they could polish the hell out of this and make it such an upgrade to it that I would come back.

667
00:51:08,710 --> 00:51:16,230
Scott: But I think this is a case where someone else will look at this, steal this idea, do it much, much better, right?

668
00:51:16,590 --> 00:51:17,809
Scott: And that's gonna be the game.

669
00:51:18,251 --> 00:51:24,150
Scott: And that will be a game to play, whereas with a Slay the Spire or a Vampire Survivor, right?

670
00:51:24,650 --> 00:51:31,130
Scott: Or, you know, an Among Us, it's like, it's gonna be, you know, for those, it's, I think, tough to beat the originals, right?

671
00:51:31,576 --> 00:51:32,309
Scott: At their own game.

672
00:51:32,490 --> 00:51:34,769
Scott: Whereas here, this is an easy to beat one.

673
00:51:35,091 --> 00:51:38,870
Scott: So, I encourage someone to try to beat it at its own.

674
00:51:38,930 --> 00:51:44,430
Rym: If you really want to beat it, maybe, like, talk to the people, talk to Zekers, the developer and publisher.

675
00:51:44,470 --> 00:51:47,510
Rym: And maybe try to hire them or partner with them in some way.

676
00:51:47,570 --> 00:51:53,810
Rym: Because they might need technological help and maybe some more professional game design help to come in from the other side.

677
00:51:54,410 --> 00:51:56,529
Rym: Because some parts are great, some parts are bad.

678
00:51:58,671 --> 00:52:05,230
Scott: But, yeah, if you got four hours and some friends and ten bucks, you could do a lot worse.

679
00:52:05,290 --> 00:52:08,750
Rym: This is easily four hours of high quality entertainment.

680
00:52:09,371 --> 00:52:11,330
Rym: And I think most people would play it twice.

681
00:52:11,791 --> 00:52:17,230
Rym: Well, we played it once because that's how we roll, but I think our friends will play it a few more times before they run out of steam.

682
00:52:21,970 --> 00:52:24,169
Rym: This has been GeekNights with Rym and Scott.

683
00:52:24,410 --> 00:52:26,890
Rym: Special thanks to DJ Pretzel for the opening theme.

684
00:52:27,090 --> 00:52:33,090
Scott: Be sure to visit our website at FrontRowCoup.com for show notes, discussion, links and more.

685
00:52:33,610 --> 00:52:36,250
Rym: Remember, GeekNights is not one, but four different shows.

686
